\p[style=Kicker]{Feature}

= Seeing the wave before it arrives

\p[style=Standfirst]{Loop detectors tell the city how fast traffic moves where they sit. Floating
cars fill the gaps. Together they let the traffic centre see a jam forming minutes before
drivers do.}

% Floating pictures and boxes are anchored in the paragraph they float beside.
\p[drop-cap=3]{\image[width=17.4cm, wrap=top-bottom, x=(center, from=margin), y=(top, from=margin),
       alt="A city skyline above three lanes of traffic"]{img/skyline.png}
\font[font=theme.major, color=theme.accent1]{E}very weekday morning a wave moves along the ring road. It is not water but
traffic: a band of slow cars that starts near the harbour bridge and travels backwards,
against the flow, at about eighteen kilometres an hour. Drivers caught in it see brake
lights appear from nowhere; drivers behind it never learn why the road suddenly slowed.}

\textbox[width=7cm, wrap=square, x=(center, from=page), y=(19cm, from=page),
         fill=theme.accent1.lighter(85%), border=(single, 1pt, theme.accent1), padding=0.3cm]{
  \p[style="Pull Quote"]{"We used to read the road like yesterday's newspaper. Now we read it
  like a weather radar."}

  \p[style="Quote Source"]{Traffic centre duty manager}
}
For decades the city knew about such waves only after the fact. The loop detectors under the
asphalt, one every half kilometre, count vehicles and measure their speed, but between two
detectors the road is dark. A wave that forms between them is invisible until it reaches the
next loop.

== Filling the gaps

The new system adds a second source: anonymous speed reports from navigation apps and
delivery fleets, the so-called floating cars. They are few, a few percent of all vehicles,
but they cover every metre of road. A mathematical model of traffic flow, the same one
engineers have used since the 1950s, ties the two sources together.

\image[width=4.2cm, wrap=tight, x=(right, from=column), distance=(left=0.3cm),
       alt="A ring of twelve sensors"]{img/sensors.png}
The model divides the road into cells of five hundred metres and predicts, every ten seconds,
how many vehicles each cell holds. Where a detector or a floating car reports, the prediction
is corrected. Where nothing reports, the model carries the picture forward. The result is a
live map of density along the whole ring, updated faster than the traffic changes.

== What the numbers say

Over four test weeks the combined estimate was a third more accurate than the detectors alone,
and almost twice as accurate in congestion, exactly where it matters most.

\table[style="Data Table", widths=(3.2cm, 2cm, 2.4cm), columns=(left, decimal, decimal),
       x=(left, from=column), y=(0.2cm, from=paragraph), distance=(right=0.4cm, bottom=0.2cm)]{
  | Condition  | Detectors | With floating cars |
  | Free flow  | 4.1       | 3.6                |
  | Slowing    | 9.8       | 6.9                |
  | Congestion | 17.2      | 9.1                |
}
The table shows the average error in vehicles per kilometre. In free flow both methods do well:
the road is empty enough that little can go wrong. As traffic thickens, the detectors alone fall
behind, because a queue can grow for minutes before it reaches a loop.

\textbox[width=100%, fill=theme.accent3.lighter(70%), padding=0.3cm]{
  \p[style=Sidebar]{\b{How a backward wave works.} When the car in front brakes, you brake a moment
  later, and the driver behind you a moment after that. The slowdown passes from car to car
  towards the back of the queue, so the wave moves upstream even though every car moves forward.}
}

== What comes next

The traffic centre now plans to use the live map for ramp metering, letting cars onto the ring
in a steady trickle when a wave is building, and to publish travel times that account for jams
that have not yet reached the next sign.

\p[style="Figure Note"]{\image[width=6.5cm, wrap=top-bottom, x=(center, from=column),
       alt="The fundamental diagram of traffic flow"]{img/fundamental-diagram.svg}
The fundamental diagram: as density rises, flow grows until the road
reaches capacity, then falls as the road jams.}

The engineers are careful not to promise miracles. The model is only as good as its picture of
how the road behaves, and roads change: lanes close, new junctions open, drivers adapt. But for
the first time the city can see the wave before its drivers do, and that is a start.
